**Verdict:** `revise` — trust strained, value high, delight clear_hit. **Blockers: 3. Concerns: 6.**

**Strongest hit —** the Rabbit Hole's falsifier: *"What would change our minds: sustained evidence that he prepares equally hard for things nobody will ever see or grade, with no audience to be credible to, would push this toward 5 or 1."* Celebrity typing normally ages into embarrassment because it states a verdict with no defeat conditions. This one hands a 2027 reader an instrument, so new evidence converts the piece into a record rather than an error. Paired with a deliberately undated opening anecdote and a ledger of closed 2005–2025 events, roughly 85% of the article survives deleting the catalyst entirely.

**Highest-priority concern —** FUTURE-R1: *"A24 releases it theatrically on October 16."* Three failures in one sentence. No year, on an evergreen page whose nearest year token is in a different sentence. Present tense, factually inverted about five weeks after the article's own date. And it is attributed to A24, which has confirmed only the month — A24's page still reads "2026 (TBD)" (packet CLM-14; my search corroborated independently), so the date can still move.

The other two blockers: **"Twenty years on"** in the close overshoots the record — twenty years before 2026 is 2006, when the Canadian Encyclopedia places him on *This Hour Has 22 Minutes* in Canada, with his earliest LA work around 2009–10; the second pass removed the anecdote's only date anchor but left the span depending on it. And the **closing quotation** names no outlet in reader-visible text, is held via a Yahoo syndication of the LA Times, and drops "I…am" and "in this" from the verified wording — the line a future reader most wants to check is the one they can least check.

